    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17150",
      "knowledge_point_id": "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959689/assets/c0bfa730f4967e8d4ece6c8d86f432d1c80a1705420fcc609ac7bfd6f5a929be.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(6,4)$, as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{52}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
